| **DIGITAL PEDAGOGY PROGRAMME  │  BUI / CON  │  Building Services ****&**** Construction** |
| --- |

**Digital Development Offer**

Building Services (BUI) and Construction (CON)  –  Teaching and Assessment Staff

**Context and rationale**

This offer has been developed in response to a referral from Megan Brookes (TLAM) and Barney (Assistant Principal) identifying a QIP Area for Improvement in BUI/CON: 

| *“Digital and technology skills development is not consistently effective across construction programmes. Learners have limited opportunities to develop and apply industry-relevant digital skills, meaning they are not fully prepared for the increasing use of technology within the construction sector, including digital planning, compliance, and modern site practices.”* |
| --- |

The construction sector is undergoing significant digital transformation. BIM (Building Information Modelling), digital site management, compliance tracking, and QR-based site systems are now standard practice across the industry. For learners to be genuinely prepared for that environment, the staff who teach and assess them need to be confident using, teaching, and modelling those skills. **This programme puts staff first — because you cannot give learners what you do not have yourself.**

Victoria Howell (Digital Lead, CON) will co-deliver all four workshops alongside the DPC. Victoria brings the industry-specific context; the DPC brings the pedagogical framework. Together this ensures the programme is grounded in the reality of construction teaching, not generic digital CPD.

**The four workshops — what every tier receives**

The four workshop sessions are consistent across Gold, Silver, and Bronze. This is not because the tiers are the same — they are not. The workshops are the DPC’s core professional offer and are not watered down regardless of the accountability tier chosen. What differs between tiers is the depth of baseline, planning, and ongoing support that surrounds the workshops. See the tier comparison on the following page.

Sessions run on Wednesday afternoons, 60–90 minutes each. Format: learn and do throughout — no slide-heavy presentations, no passive note-taking. Every session produces something tangible that staff leave with and can use the following week.

| **Session** | **Title** | **What we do** | **What you leave with** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **1** | **Digital Capabilities and Action Plan  —  with Accessibility** | We begin with where staff actually are, not where we assume they are. Working from individual Jisc Discovery Tool profiles (or self-declaration, depending on tier), each person identifies their top two digital priorities. Victoria leads a short industry context session: what digital skills matter in BUI/CON, what learners will need in the sector, and what the QIP AFI is actually asking us to change. The DPC introduces the Digital Learning Pyramid and the Accessibility by Design standard. We run the Microsoft Accessibility Checker together on one resource each person has brought. No exceptions — every resource gets checked. | Completed personal digital action plan (two priorities, three actions each, named completion date). At least one resource audited and corrected for accessibility before leaving the session. Digital Learning Pyramid position identified. Baseline recorded. |
| **2** | **Teams Learning Environments and Inclusive Practice** | The Teams environment is the digital classroom. This session treats it as such. We audit the BUI/CON Teams spaces together using the Digital Health Check — channel structure, naming conventions, Assignments, file accessibility, and learner-facing layout. Victoria contextualises this for construction delivery: how a well-structured Teams space supports digital site diary submissions, resource sharing for off-site learners, and compliance evidence collection. The DPC leads on inclusive practice: Live Captions as default, Immersive Reader for learners who need it, accessible file formats. We rebuild or restructure at least one Teams channel in the session. | Digital Health Check completed for own Teams area — results shared with HoA. At least one Teams channel restructured to Health Check standard. Inclusive practice checklist completed: captions, Immersive Reader, accessible files. |
| **3** | **Assessment for Learning and Capturing Live Modelling for Independent Learning** | This session addresses the most important question in the QIP AFI: are learners developing and applying digital skills, or just being taught them? The difference is in how assessment and modelling work. Victoria leads on industry modelling: how expert practitioners demonstrate digital site tools, compliance software, and BIM workflows in a way that learners can observe, replicate, and then do independently. We discuss screen recording, short expert videos saved to Teams, and peer modelling between learners. The DPC leads on Assessment for Learning (AfL): Microsoft Forms for quick knowledge checks, Assignments with rubrics that include digital skill criteria, and Teams Analytics to see which learners are engaging with digital resources and which are not. We build one formative check together in the session. | One formative digital assessment built and tested in Microsoft Forms or Assignments. A modelling strategy agreed for one unit — how staff will show, and then hand over, a specific digital skill to learners. Teams Analytics reviewed. At least one learner engagement gap identified with a planned response. |
| **4** | **Putting It Into Practice  —  with AI Copilot introduction (if time allows)** | The final session is a consolidation and a commitment. Staff bring one piece of work they have changed or created since Session 1 — a resource, an assessment, a Teams environment, a modelling video. We share these as a group. This is the Teach Meet moment built into the programme. Victoria and the DPC facilitate peer discussion: what worked, what did not, what needs more support. The DPC captures this as coaching evidence. Where time allows, the DPC introduces Microsoft Copilot as a teaching and planning support tool — not as an AI strategy, but as a practical time-saver for lesson planning, resource adaptation, and feedback drafting. Staff leave with one Copilot prompt they have written and tested themselves. | Personal development plan updated: what has changed, what is next, what support is needed. One piece of changed practice shared with the group — logged as Teach Meet contribution. Copilot prompt written and tested (if time allows). Signposted to Microsoft Learn Copilot pathway for self-directed continuation. |

**The three tiers — what differs between them**

The four workshops are identical in every tier. The difference is what surrounds them: the depth of baseline, the structure of the HoA planning conversation, and the rigour of the ongoing support and accountability. Each tier produces a QIP-credible evidence trail, but Gold produces the most complete one.

|  | **GOLD** Full structured development programme | **SILVER** Self-declaration baseline with structured workshops | **BRONZE** Responsive, informal with light-touch baseline |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Baseline** | Jisc Discovery Tool (Teaching Staff FE) — individual results private, group code set up by DPC. Profile used to personalise action plan. | DPC-designed self-declaration survey aligned to Digital Learning Pyramid. Confidence scores per skill, per user. Scores known to DPC for planning. | Informal needs conversation with HoA or team. Staff write a “what I hope to get from these sessions” statement at Session 1. |
| **HoA planning session** | DPC meets with HoA (and TLAM if appropriate) to refine the BUI/CON Industry Digital Skills map, agree learner baseline standards using Century, and build a staff action plan around and beyond the four workshops. | DPC meets briefly with HoA to share self-declaration results and agree the focus for the workshops. No industry framework refinement at this stage. | DPC meets with HoA or goes direct to team. Responsive to what the team identifies as priorities. |
| **Industry Digital Skills framework** | HoA and DPC collaboratively refine the BUI/CON section of the college Digital Skills Framework. This becomes the benchmark for what learners should be able to do in this area, and the standard against which staff confidence is measured. | DPC uses the existing Digital Skills Framework as reference. HoA reviews but does not formally refine it at this stage. | Not addressed at this stage. Can be developed if momentum builds after the workshops. |
| **Ongoing coaching support** | 1:1 coaching for any staff member available on referral from Digital Lead or TLAM, triggered by workshop learning or HoA conversation. Supported learning walks and live coaching sessions offered where a staff member is implementing a new approach. DPC available for group follow-up between sessions. | 1:1 coaching available on request. DPC signposts to Microsoft Learn pathways matched to self-declaration priorities. | 1:1 coaching available on request. DPC responds informally to individual needs. |
| **Learner digital skills** | HoA and DPC agree a Century baseline plan for learners: Initial Assessment deployed, Specialist Digital Skills map agreed for BUI/CON, progress reviewed termly. Staff and learner development aligned. | Discussed in HoA meeting. Century plan noted as a recommended next step for 2026/27. | Not addressed at this stage. |
| **Check-in and progress review** | End-of-programme check-in with DPC (30 mins, in person or Teams): Jisc profile revisited, Digital Learning Pyramid position reviewed, updated action plan agreed, next steps documented. Written progress summary produced for TLAM and HoA. | End-of-programme reflection statement submitted by each staff member. DPC produces group summary for TLAM. | DPC writes a brief session reflection. Staff complete a short self-reflection form after Session 4. |
| **QIP evidence produced** | Jisc baseline + end profile, personal action plans (signed), Digital Health Check before/after, Teach Meet contribution log, progress review summary, coaching log entries. Full before/after story. | Self-declaration before/after confidence scores, action plans, Health Check, reflection statements, group summary. Clear progress narrative. | Session attendance, staff self-reflections, brief DPC summary. Activity evidence. Strengthened by adding a 5-minute pre/post confidence rating in Sessions 1 and 4. |
| **Victoria Howell involvement** | Co-delivers all four workshops. Leads the industry skills context session in Session 1. Continues as Digital Lead running termly Health Checks after the programme ends. | Co-delivers all four workshops. Continues Health Check role. | Co-delivers all four workshops. Health Check role continues. |

| **My recommendation** Gold is the right offer for BUI/CON. The QIP AFI is specific about learners not being prepared for industry digital practice — that requires a credible before/after evidence chain and a plan that extends beyond four sessions. Gold provides that. The HoA planning session and the industry framework refinement are also things the programme needs to do for this area regardless of which tier is chosen. It makes sense to do them properly, once, and have them count. The Jisc Discovery Tool setup can be completed this week. All other elements are already in place. The commitment from BUI/CON is 10 staff, four Wednesday afternoons, and a brief HoA conversation beforehand. The return is a documented development programme with measurable impact, a stronger Digital Lead in Victoria, and a QIP AFI that has a credible response. |
